Thomas Kerber: kind of service of runtime type representation that lets us do that polymorphism type script side. I think this case is slightly different, because the descriptors

156
00:23:10.920 --> 00:23:15.759
Thomas Kerber: Again, only contain information for creating…

157
00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:20.180
Thomas Kerber: Field-aligned binary alignments, and we actually want

158
00:23:20.750 --> 00:23:26.339
Thomas Kerber: different information here. We don't care what type the ledger sees something as, but what

159
00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:31.489
Thomas Kerber: type… you know, we actually want more granular type information at this point.

160
00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:36.609
Thomas Kerber: But yeah, just to point out, we don't…

161
00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:40.680
Thomas Kerber: Necess… just because it's polymorphic,

162
00:23:40.790 --> 00:23:45.310
Thomas Kerber: in a way that we can't represent in TypeScript is not necessarily a problem there.

163
00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:55.949
Joseph Denman: Yeah. So, I guess I'll stop screen sharing. That's the gist of it.

164
00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:01.570
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Excellent. Thank you for that.

165
00:24:01.870 --> 00:24:06.450
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I do think directionally that is the right way that we've… for us to move.

166
00:24:15.120 --> 00:24:19.050
Kent Dybvig: I definitely like checking on… in, constructors.

167
00:24:19.500 --> 00:24:20.280
Kent Dybvig: M.

168
00:24:20.380 --> 00:24:26.570
Kent Dybvig: Of course, we need other checks as well, but checking in the constructor is much better than checking on every access point.

169
00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:37.459
Joseph Denman: Yeah, we have this… we try to dummy-proof, I guess, JavaScript in various ways.

170
00:24:37.830 --> 00:24:39.210
Joseph Denman: As well, it's…

171
00:24:41.080 --> 00:24:44.829
Joseph Denman: I don't know how necessary that is in the long run, but it's a convention that, you know.

172
00:24:45.550 --> 00:24:46.660
Joseph Denman: Don't we set?

173
00:24:48.350 --> 00:24:51.730
Kent Dybvig: You mean as far as doing… doing the runtime checks that are…

174
00:24:52.100 --> 00:24:52.570
Joseph Denman: Yeah.

175
00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:55.369
Kent Dybvig: Are implied by the static checks, if…

176
00:24:56.290 --> 00:25:08.109
Kent Dybvig: if people, if people are in the habit of using anys in their TypeScript code, or just writing straight JavaScript code, or whatever, yeah, I think that's pretty… pretty useful for us. I… I do think we have a…

177
00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:20.619
Kent Dybvig: There is a question about whether we should be doing that as proactively as we're doing, because, you know, you get a very long array, we're checking every element to make sure it's the right type.

178
00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:30.179
Kent Dybvig: And you may only be referencing element 17. Now, if you're doing a proof, it probably doesn't matter, but if this is, like, a private circuit.

179
00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:35.100
Kent Dybvig: You know, this is a circuit that is pure.

180
00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:44.099
Kent Dybvig: Then it's doing unnecessary work that'll… But it isn't… isn't, fully absorbed by the proof.

181
00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:47.670
Kent Dybvig: Or overshadowed by the proof.

182
00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:50.140
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, Thomas?

183
00:25:50.140 --> 00:25:54.700
Thomas Kerber: This is a… As a comment, the ledger side…

184
00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:57.799
Thomas Kerber: Which may or may not be relevant to you.

185
00:25:57.970 --> 00:26:06.579
Thomas Kerber: We assume that you are calling it in a type-safe way with the correct TypeScript types.

186
00:26:06.790 --> 00:26:11.379
Thomas Kerber: And we know you can get around that. If you get around that, then…

187
00:26:13.040 --> 00:26:17.770
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, what happens is your fault. That's kind of our perspective.

188
00:26:20.790 --> 00:26:27.649
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, and we could… we could certainly do that. We could… we could… we could have it disabled. I think it's been a great safety net.

189
00:26:27.810 --> 00:26:29.369
Kent Dybvig: Up to this point.

190
00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:33.729
Kent Dybvig: I think it's a great safety net to have as at least a debugging option.

191
00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:35.890
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, I think I…

192
00:26:35.890 --> 00:26:37.690
Kent Dybvig: We certainly have choices.

193
00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:44.330
Thomas Kerber: Yeah, our constraints on the ledger are different, because, it is…

194
00:26:45.020 --> 00:26:51.289
Thomas Kerber: difficult and annoying to check those types at runtime in WASM.

195
00:26:51.750 --> 00:26:58.700
Thomas Kerber: Especially because the… Wrapper library does part of that for you, but not all of it.

196
00:27:05.200 --> 00:27:13.950
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I was just gonna point out that this is exactly the problem that so-called gradual type systems have, which are type systems that try to mix typed and untyped code.

197
00:27:14.690 --> 00:27:21.340
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Where for us, the untyped code is the JavaScript code, which just doesn't have compact-type system.

198
00:27:22.020 --> 00:27:29.160
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And, it's… there's a tension between how deep your checking goes, because it's costly.

199
00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:38.810
Kent Dybvig: It's not just that, there's also a tension, if you pass…

200
00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:43.230
Kent Dybvig: Right now, if you pass a mutable data structure

201
00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:48.980
Kent Dybvig: to… say you get… say you say, I have a… I have a circuit,

202
00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:57.630
Kent Dybvig: that takes a struct of some sort, and you're past a valid object. You say, cool, that object is valid.

203
00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.729
Kent Dybvig: Then you, in turn, pass that to a witness.

204
00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:07.889
Kent Dybvig: Then that witness mangles the object, removes a field, say.

205
00:28:09.150 --> 00:28:12.639
Kent Dybvig: We ought to be checking it when that witness returns. We're not.

206
00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:18.750
Kent Dybvig: So, really, the safest thing would be for us to check every access, rather than

207
00:28:19.550 --> 00:28:25.110
Kent Dybvig: Rather than every entry and return point. There is even… you know.

208
00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:32.559
Kent Dybvig: I don't know what… how much concurrency is available in these systems right now, if any.

209
00:28:32.980 --> 00:28:36.339
Kent Dybvig: I can well imagine a day where there's… where it's common.

210
00:28:36.690 --> 00:28:40.540
Kent Dybvig: And so there's even a possibility that things change out from under you.

211
00:28:42.910 --> 00:28:48.009
Kent Dybvig: Which is very difficult to check for. In general, you have to have a lock or something like that.

212
00:28:49.350 --> 00:28:57.130
Kent Dybvig: But I think the safest thing would actually be for us to move the checks to the access points. Every array reference, every struct reference.

213
00:28:58.690 --> 00:29:04.610
Kent Dybvig: And then if we feel like there's too much overhead in doing that, then…

214
00:29:06.670 --> 00:29:12.540
Kent Dybvig: And to be clear, there could be a lot less overhead doing that than checking the whole array proactively.

215
00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:19.870
Kent Dybvig: If we feel like there's too much overhead in doing that, though, we can provide a switch for people to disable it.

216
00:29:25.490 --> 00:29:30.060
Kent Dybvig: I just think we… we have a… we have a… we have a language here that's supposed to be…

217
00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:40.209
Kent Dybvig: safer than… Than the ordinary language running on… running in… in… in the browser.

218
00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:46.359
Kent Dybvig: So I always felt like it was a bit of an obligation for us to be

219
00:29:46.820 --> 00:29:49.299
Kent Dybvig: Enforcing those types.

220
00:29:52.480 --> 00:29:54.500
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I… I agree with that.

221
00:29:56.120 --> 00:30:06.520
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I worked on a language called Dart. Dart1 had a checked mode, which was exactly that. Everything was checked, and then it had unchecked mode, and the idea…

222
00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:17.600
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Was that you would run all your tests in checked mode, and then you would deploy in unchecked mode. But we eventually just abandoned that because it was less safe than having a better type system.

223
00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:21.490
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): I don't know the answer.

224
00:30:21.490 --> 00:30:28.180
Kent Dybvig: You abandon the checks? You abandoned the runtime checks? We made a… we came up with a sound type system for Dart.

225
00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:33.100
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): That was always… that had some runtime checks, but not the full…

226
00:30:33.750 --> 00:30:34.640
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, gotcha.

227
00:30:35.180 --> 00:30:43.859
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Yeah. And the other problem with gradual type systems is when you have higher order values, like functional values, you'll have to just basically wrap them.

228
00:30:44.360 --> 00:30:49.160
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Because you don't… you can't… you can't tell where they're going to be applied, and what you have to check… you always have to check.

229
00:30:51.090 --> 00:30:55.429
Kent Dybvig: Right. Unless you can do the escape analysis and prove that they don't actually know what is…

230
00:30:56.440 --> 00:30:57.160
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): doom.

231
00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:03.899
Kent Dybvig: Yeah, so I should probably create a story for moving the checks to the access points.

232
00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:09.400
Kent Dybvig: And if people feel strongly about it, I can add a story for,

233
00:31:09.640 --> 00:31:12.070
Kent Dybvig: for disabling the checks, though I…

234
00:31:13.500 --> 00:31:18.480
Kent Dybvig: I'd like to wait till we see that it's actually a performance problem before I get excited about doing that.

235
00:31:20.050 --> 00:31:21.140
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): That sounds right.

236
00:31:22.110 --> 00:31:26.060
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): So I think… I think, I think your proposal is directionally exactly what we want, Joe.

237
00:31:26.190 --> 00:31:27.969
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Type-safe constructors.

238
00:31:28.940 --> 00:31:34.239
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): And definitely, we want to allow people who just want to do the right thing to make it easy for them.

239
00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:41.269
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Is there anything else anyone wants to add to the agenda?

240
00:31:47.710 --> 00:31:50.080
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Okay, that sounds like a… sounds like a no.

241
00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:53.690
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): We'll give everybody a half hour back.

242
00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:56.760
Kevin Millikin (Shielded Technologies): Happy hacking, everybody. Thanks for this.

